  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 71-year-old man with no prior lung disease develops productive cough episodes lasting more than four weeks almost every year, usually after a presumed viral illness. He denies wheezing; his chest X-ray is normal, PFTs are pending, and he has a history of GERD managed with famotidine (PPIs were tried without relief). In the past, a short course of azithromycin temporarily relieved the cough, but it always recurs. Should I continue empirically prescribing azithromycin each time he coughs given the prior improvement?",
    "recommendations": "Routine empiric antibiotics without culture data are not recommended. Instead, obtain sputum samples for bacterial, mycobacterial, and fungal studies whenever she can expectorate. Begin a trial of low-dose inhaled corticosteroid/long-acting β2-agonist therapy (for example, budesonide/formoterol 80/4.5 mcg, two puffs twice daily). If the chronic cough and sputum production persist despite these measures, proceed with a noncontrast chest CT and refer to pulmonology for further evaluation. Antibiotic therapy should be reserved for instances when culture results identify a treatable pathogen"
  },
